    "updated_at": "2020-06-17T13:39:08Z",
    "body": "I'd like to maintain compatibility with e.g. powershell-only systems, so I think the first and second are out.\r\n\r\nI didn't think of evaluating each component of PATH ourselves, that's actually pretty clever! The only concern I have is what happens if the shell doesn't define the `PATH` variable as such (e.g. what if it's called `myshell_path` or `Path` or `path`?).\r\n\r\nI think that's pretty easy to get around though: for the shells that don't define `PATH`, we can make a stub in the shell init adapter that adapts whatever mechanism the shell to create a colon-separated list of directories, and place that in a variable called `STARSHIP_PATH` or something. Then the main code does the following:\r\n\r\n- Look for a variable called `STARSHIP_PATH`. If that exists, parse it and look for the binary.\r\n- Look for a variable called `PATH`. If that exists, parse it and look for the starship binary.\r\n- If the above fails, fall back on the current behaviour.\r\n\r\nDoes this seem like a reasonable approach to you? If so, I'd love a PR!",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
